# Overview
#
# `various` inflates a count without giving it, so the reader learns
# neither the items nor their number. It often adds nothing (`handles
# various edge cases` says no more than `handles edge cases`). The fix
# names the items, or deletes the word when the sentence lists them. The
# rule sits at Optional, because the fix makes few sentences clearer,
# and because `various` also means `different`.
#
# Expression
#
# The filter takes `various` before any word but `different` and narrows
# the finding back to `various`.
#
# Related Rules
#
# The Recommended ProseQuantity holds the other phrases that inflate a
# count. `various` sits apart from them, because it also means
# `different`.
#
# The Required ProseRedundantPair reports `various different`, so the
# filter skips `various` before `different`.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 119 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 18 of 20 sampled
# hits lost no meaning without `various`. Only 7 of the 18 read clearer.
# The 2 misfires meant `different` (`various sizes`). Deleting `various`
# there changes the meaning, as in `users from various backgrounds`.
expr main [<(various !different), various>]
let message "Vague quantity. Name the items, or delete `various` when the items follow."
let description "Flags `various`, which inflates a count without giving it."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "The parser handles three edge cases."
allows "The tools use various different formats."
